<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Vienna (1529)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1529">1529</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-habsburg-wars">Ottoman–Habsburg wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1529 was the first Ottoman attempt to capture the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> capital, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a>. Bad weather, long supply lines and a determined defence forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> to withdraw. Accounts of the Ottoman Empire place it among Suleiman's major campaigns, marking the limit of Ottoman expansion in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In August 1526 Suleiman decisively defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-hungary">Louis II of Hungary</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a>, and the childless Louis died. Archduke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-austria">Ferdinand I of Austria</a> claimed the throne but won recognition only in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. A noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a>, challenged him and was recognised as king by Suleiman in return for accepting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> status. Hungary became divided into three zones: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-hungary">Ottoman Hungary</a> and the Principality of Transylvania. Ferdinand captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> in 1527 but lost it in 1529 to an Ottoman counter-attack.</p><p>Historians offer conflicting interpretations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>' long-term aims. Some argue that Suleiman wished to assert control over all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a>, and others that he intended to use Hungary as a base for further invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the spring of 1529 Suleiman mustered a large army in Ottoman Bulgaria and launched his campaign on 10 May. Contemporary chroniclers gave figures from 120,000 to over 300,000, but modern estimates put his combat force at the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> at between 75,000 and 100,000. Heavy spring rains bogged down his heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, which had to be abandoned, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> were lost in large numbers and sickness spread among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> on 8 September.</p><p>The defenders, no more than 21,000, were commanded by Niklas Graf Salm, a seventy-year-old German mercenary, with German Landsknecht pikemen, Spanish harquebusiers sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and 2,000 Serbian hussars under Pavle Bakić. Salm strengthened the old walls and levelled buildings to clear room for defences. The siege lasted from 27 September to 15 October. The defenders raided Ottoman sappers and blew up mines, and on 6 October a force of 8,000 men attacked the Ottoman mining works, destroying many tunnels at heavy cost. A final assault on 14 October was beaten back. On 15 October Suleiman called off the siege, and the army returned through snow and mud, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 16 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>During the campaign Ottoman troops pillaged the countryside around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, killed many civilians and took survivors as slaves, an estimated 20,000 people, few of whom returned. Salm, wounded in the last assault, died on 4 May 1530.</p><p>The campaign had mixed results. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> returned to the control of the Ottoman vassal Zápolya, and the devastation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands weakened Ferdinand, but Suleiman could not force him into open battle. The failure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> began about 150 years of military tension between the Habsburgs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, which culminated in a second siege of Vienna in 1683. A further campaign in 1532 was stalled at Güns by Nikola Jurišić and turned back. The two campaigns marked the limit of Ottoman logistical capability to field large armies deep in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> at the time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Vienna (1529)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
